%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a0001cc40633e2242a75f7015c8a9162, type: 3}
  m_Name: Quest1_Level4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  title: "Quest 1 \xB7 Level 4"
  lockZoomOut: 0
  graphPrecipOnly: 0
  graphVegVariables: 1
  lockedScenario: -1
  lockedGraphVariable: -1
  allowSideBySide: 1
  allowDifferentCubes: 0
  opening: Looking at climate can only do so much to explain ecosystem processes
    and wildfire risk. Another important factor to consider is vegetation, and how
    that couples with climate in different locations to create differences in vegetation
    cover, vegetation behavior, and vegetation response to fire.
  ending: 
  defaultOptions:
  - North
  - South
  - High
  - Low
  - Riparian
  questions:
  - prompt: "This zone has the largest year-to-year variation in plant\u2019s primary
      productivity (or energy generation), above ground biomass, respiration, and
      transpiration. It is characterized by an average elevation within the watershed."
    options:
    - North
    - South
    - High
    - Low
    - Riparian
    correctIndex: 0
    graphSelect: 0
    correctYearsByScenario: []
  - prompt: "This zone has the lowest amount of above ground biomass, and is considered
      the least productive in terms of net primary productivity due to the fact that
      this zone is mostly composed of grass. This zone receives more solar radiation
      as it receives the most direct sunlight of all of the zones. More sunlight
      and radiation can lead to increased temperatures, increased evaporation, and
      less water available in the soil for plants\u3002"
    options:
    - North
    - South
    - High
    - Low
    - Riparian
    correctIndex: 1
    graphSelect: 0
    correctYearsByScenario: []
  - prompt: This zone is one where the plants have access to water from a stream.
      This zone is the only zone with coast live oaks. Coast live oaks average 10-15
      meters in height, compared to chaparral where for mature shrubs, the average
      height is 2-4 meters. Water is not a limiting factor in this zone, leading
      to increased biomass and potentially taller plants.
    options:
    - North
    - South
    - High
    - Low
    - Riparian
    correctIndex: 4
    graphSelect: 0
    correctYearsByScenario: []
  - prompt: "This zone has, on average, a lower above ground biomass for the chaparral
      shrubs. In this zone rainfall does not determine how much access the vegetation
      has to water, as water naturally travels \u201Cdown slope\u201D to lower elevation
      patches like this one. It can also be categorized as the least \u201Cthirsty\u201D
      for its low transpiration levels."
    options:
    - North
    - South
    - High
    - Low
    - Riparian
    correctIndex: 3
    graphSelect: 0
    correctYearsByScenario: []
  - prompt: ' This zone has the most above ground biomass in comparison to the other
      zones, and consistent vegetation height as no fire or other disturbances occur
      at this elevation level in any of our simulations. The amount of access the
      vegetation has to water is very dependent on the amount of rainfall this zone
      accumulates; however, it does accumulate the most rainfall out of all the zones.
      More moisture is available at this level, so plants are not constrained by
      water for engaging in evaporation and transpiration.'
    options:
    - North
    - South
    - High
    - Low
    - Riparian
    correctIndex: 2
    graphSelect: 0
    correctYearsByScenario: []
